## 5. Roadmap

Ordered by what unblocks the most. Issue numbers are the demand signal.

### Phase 1 — make it render (built, awaiting live verification)

The goal is a graph on screen against a real repository.

- [x] `src/git/repository.ts` — discover repositories: the `vscode.git`
      extension API first, workspace folder scan as fallback, honouring
      `maxDepthOfRepoSearch`.
- [x] `src/view/protocol.ts` — typed request/response messages.
- [x] `src/view/panel.ts` — the webview panel: strict CSP with a nonce,
      `retainContextWhenHidden`, state restore across reloads.
- [x] `webview/render/graph.ts` — SVG renderer consuming `GraphLayout`.
- [x] `webview/render/table.ts` — virtualised commit table; only visible rows in
      the DOM. **Sticky header (#132).**
- [x] `webview/style.css` — themed entirely from VS Code CSS variables so light,
      dark and high-contrast all work without a per-theme branch.
- [x] `src/extension.ts` — activation, command registration, status bar item.

**Fix while building, do not retrofit** — these are why users are leaving today:

- [x] #903 — SCM title icon disappears. `when` clauses must not depend on
      context keys that are never set; set `gitGraphNext.enabled` explicitly on
      activation.
- [x] #794 — `Cannot read properties of null (reading 'rootUri')`. Never assume
      the git extension has a repository for the active editor.
- [x] #873 — right-click broken after VS Code 1.97. Own context menu inside the
      webview; do not rely on native menu behaviour.
- [x] #863 — context menu absent in VSCodium. No dependency on
      Marketplace-only or proprietary-build APIs.

### Phase 2 — read-only power features

Structurally already supported by `LogFilter` / `layoutGraph`; they need UI.

- [x] #70 — filter the graph by file or folder → `LogFilter.paths`
- [x] #171 — filter by author → `LogFilter.authors`
- [x] #760 — filter displayed branches → `LogFilter.branches`
- [ ] #360 — exclude branches by glob → `LogFilter.excludeGlobs`
- [ ] #591 — arbitrary extra `git log` arguments → `LogFilter.extraArgs`
- [ ] #207 — persistent branches get a dedicated column → `pinnedBranches`
- [ ] #254 — branch colour on all commits → first-parent colour inheritance
- [ ] #465 — filter by tag
- [x] #147 — advanced search
- [ ] #387 — collapsible graph sections
- [ ] #313 — richer issue linking
- [ ] #475 — display git notes
- [ ] #598 — rich diff for Jupyter notebooks
- [x] #781 — minimal graph in the side panel (session 3)

### Phase 3 — write actions

`GitRefReader.readState()` already detects interrupted operations, which is what
makes the continue/abort affordances possible.

- [ ] Checkout, branch create/delete/rename, merge, rebase, cherry-pick, revert,
      reset, stash apply/pop/drop, tag create/delete, push, pull, fetch.
- [ ] #182 — multi-commit operations (select a range, act on it)
- [ ] #410 — create fixup / squash, and autosquash, from the commit context menu
- [ ] #113 — complete rebase support
- [ ] #757 — interactive rebase driven from the VS Code editor
- [ ] #587 — autosquash option in the rebase dialog
- [ ] #519 — `merge --abort`
- [ ] #184 — delete several branches at once
- [ ] #538 — create and apply patches

### Phase 4 — release

- [ ] Extension icon, README with screenshots, CHANGELOG.
- [ ] Publisher account; `vsce package` in CI.
- [ ] Performance pass against a repository with 100k+ commits.
- [ ] #675 — restore the graph tab after a VS Code restart.

